1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,757
Customers rarely quote a transaction ID.

2
00:00:03,807 --> 00:00:07,285
When a message could match several payments,

3
00:00:07,335 --> 00:00:13,941
an assistant that guesses can give a correct-sounding answer about the wrong money.

4
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,266
Finbot is built not to guess.

5
00:00:17,107 --> 00:00:18,277
Here's an example.

6
00:00:18,327 --> 00:00:20,783
A customer of a fictional bank types:

7
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:25,253
"My two-hundred-dollar payment hasn't arrived." No date, no payee,

8
00:00:25,303 --> 00:00:26,472
no transaction ID.

9
00:00:26,522 --> 00:00:29,926
Their account has four two-hundred-dollar payments,

10
00:00:29,976 --> 00:00:32,500
so the amount alone rules nothing out.

11
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:37,512
Finbot keeps a probability for each record the customer is allowed to see,

12
00:00:37,562 --> 00:00:42,525
scores each question it could ask by how much it would narrow things down,

13
00:00:42,575 --> 00:00:46,859
and asks the best one: was it a card payment or a bank transfer?

14
00:00:47,708 --> 00:00:50,013
The customer picks bank transfer.

15
00:00:50,063 --> 00:00:55,010
Asked for the date, they name the payee instead, and Finbot uses that.

16
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:58,080
One record now fits what the customer said,

17
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:01,792
but a probability never selects a record on its own.

18
00:01:01,842 --> 00:01:04,004
The customer has to confirm it.

19
00:01:05,345 --> 00:01:11,088
Now the confirmed record is fetched again, and a language model drafts the reply.

20
00:01:11,138 --> 00:01:13,806
Here, the draft adds a processing fee.

21
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:17,382
Plain code, not another model, pulls every amount,

22
00:01:17,432 --> 00:01:22,246
status and ID out of the draft and looks for each one in the record.

23
00:01:22,296 --> 00:01:27,324
The fee isn't there, so the draft is rejected, and the reason is shown.

24
00:01:27,374 --> 00:01:30,685
The repair repeats the fee and is rejected too,

25
00:01:30,735 --> 00:01:35,764
so the answer is built from the record itself: the transfer is pending.

26
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:43,641
If the customer corrects themselves,

27
00:01:43,691 --> 00:01:48,795
the earlier answer is withdrawn and Finbot starts again from the new record.

28
00:01:50,053 --> 00:01:55,474
We tested this on 224 real Banking77 questions with the reference removed,

29
00:01:55,524 --> 00:01:59,097
with simulated customers from two model families.

30
00:01:59,147 --> 00:02:04,716
Among eight records, one well-chosen question puts the right record first in

31
00:02:04,766 --> 00:02:10,187
about seven of ten conversations, against two in ten for a fixed question.

32
00:02:10,237 --> 00:02:15,066
Confirmation keeps wrong-record selections at two to four percent,

33
00:02:15,116 --> 00:02:19,058
against over twenty if Finbot acted on its best guess.

34
00:02:20,208 --> 00:02:24,820
Try it in your browser, or run it offline with no API key.
